 text:[
 "Written as the soundtrack to Joe Massot's psychedelic film <em>Wonderwall</em>, this was the first solo album by any Beatle and the first LP on the band's new Apple label. Harrison split the work between London, where Liverpool's Remo Four and friends such as Eric Clapton and Ringo Starr cut the Western cues, and Bombay, where he directed a group of Indian classical musicians in short, raga-based miniatures.",
 "<strong>Then:</strong> reviews were mixed – Barry Miles in <em>International Times</em> called it a “gentle human record”, while <em>Melody Maker</em> felt much of it had little point away from the pictures. It reached No. 49 in the US.",
 "<strong>Now:</strong> it is treated as a hidden gem. <em>Uncut</em>'s Richard Williams called it “an exploded diagram of a Beatles album… a treat from start to finish”, and the Indian sessions also produced the backing for the Beatles' “The Inner Light”."],
