 text:[
 "Harrison's second album – and one of the two launch titles of Apple's short-lived experimental offshoot, Zapple – consists of just two long pieces made on an early Moog modular synthesizer, one of the first to reach Britain. The cover reproduces Harrison's own naive painting.",
 "<strong>Then:</strong> the <em>NME</em> called it “for intellectuals”, and <em>Rolling Stone</em>'s Ed Ward thought Harrison had “done quite well learning his way around his new Moog… but he's still got a way to go”. It spent two weeks on the US chart, peaking at No. 191.",
 "<strong>Now:</strong> opinion is split between curiosity and respect. AllMusic's Richard Ginell argues the pieces “can hold their own and then some” against more serious electronic composers, given that early synthesizers could play only one note at a time; others still hear it as an expensive toy being explored."],
