 text:[
 "Moved by his friend Ravi Shankar's plea for refugees from the war in East Pakistan, Harrison organised two shows in a single day at Madison Square Garden – the prototype for every charity concert since. He coaxed a reclusive Bob Dylan onto the stage, reunited with Ringo, and fronted a big band that included Clapton, Leon Russell, Billy Preston and Badfinger.",
 "<strong>Then:</strong> the triple-LP box was hailed as an event. <em>Melody Maker</em>'s Richard Williams wrote “If you buy only one LP in 1972, make it this one”, and <em>The Guardian</em> called the concert “the greatest act of magnanimity rock music has yet achieved”. It was No. 1 in Britain and won the Grammy for Album of the Year.",
 "<strong>Now:</strong> remembered as the moment that, in Ian Inglis's words, “established the artistic legitimacy of the charity album” – though later CD listeners noticed the rough edges of the 1971 recording."],
 beatles:[{kind:"quote",who:"Paul McCartney, 1971",text:"“The reason I couldn't do it was because it would mean that all the world's press would scream that the Beatles had got back together again… I didn't really fancy it anyway.”",src:"https://www.beatlesbible.com/1971/08/01/live-concert-for-bangla-desh/",srcName:"Melody Maker, via Beatles Bible"},
  {kind:"fact",who:"Ringo Starr",text:"Ringo flew in from a film shoot in Spain to drum and sing “It Don't Come Easy” – famously forgetting some of its lyrics. John Lennon was invited but did not take part."}],
