 text:[
 "Harrison's leaner, self-produced follow-up swaps Spector's wall of sound for a small, intimate band and some of his finest slide guitar. The songs pull between the spiritual and the material: devotional pieces sit beside “Sue Me, Sue You Blues”, a wry look at the Beatles' legal break-up. He gave the publishing rights for most of the songs to his new Material World Charitable Foundation.",
 "<strong>Then:</strong> <em>Rolling Stone</em>'s Stephen Holden called it a “pop classic” and “profoundly seductive”, but British critics were tougher – the <em>NME</em>'s Tony Tyler found it “so damn holy I could scream”. It went gold within two days in the US and spent five weeks at No. 1, with “Give Me Love” on top of the singles chart at the same time.",
 "<strong>Now:</strong> often ranked as his second-best album. The 2024 50th-anniversary edition put it back in the top ten in Scotland and the top 20 in Germany."],
 beatles:[{kind:"fact",who:"Ringo Starr",text:"Ringo shares the drumming with Jim Keltner."},{kind:"fact",who:"Paul McCartney",text:"“Sue Me, Sue You Blues” was Harrison's comment on McCartney's 1971 High Court action to dissolve the Beatles' partnership."}],
 sources:[["Wikipedia",W("Living_in_the_Material_World")],["AllMusic",AM("living-in-the-material-world-bonus-tracks-dvd-mw0000573364")]]
