 released:"19 November 1976 (US)", label:"Dark Horse", producer:"George Harrison, with Tom Scott",
 recorded:"May – September 1976 · FPSHOT (Friar Park), Henley",
 tag:"A sunny comeback at age 33⅓",
 charts:{UK:"35",US:"11",NO:"17"}, single:"“This Song” – US No. 25 · “Crackerbox Palace” – US No. 19",
 ratings:"AllMusic 3.5/5 · Blender 4/5 · Rolling Stone Album Guide 3.5/5 · Uncut 7/10",
 tracks:{"Side one":["Woman Don't You Cry for Me","Dear One","Beautiful Girl","This Song","See Yourself"],
  "Side two":["It's What You Value","True Love (Cole Porter)","Pure Smokey","Crackerbox Palace","Learning How to Love You"]},
 personnel:[["George Harrison","vocals, electric & acoustic guitars, synthesizers, percussion"],["Tom Scott","saxophones, flute, lyricon"],
  ["Richard Tee","piano, organ, Fender Rhodes"],["Willie Weeks","bass"],["Alvin Taylor","drums"],["Billy Preston","piano, organ, synthesizer"],
  ["David Foster","Fender Rhodes, clavinet"],["Gary Wright","keyboards"],["Emil Richards","marimba"],["Eric Idle","voices on “This Song”"]],
 text:[
 "The first album on his own Dark Horse label, named after his age and the speed of an LP. Things went wrong along the way: hepatitis stopped the sessions for much of the summer, A&M sued over the late delivery and the label moved to Warner Bros., and he lost the “My Sweet Lord” plagiarism case. He answered that last one with “This Song”, a cheerful send-up with comic interjections from Monty Python's Eric Idle, who also directed two of the album's joke videos.",
 "<strong>Then:</strong> his best reviews since 1970. <em>Billboard</em> called it “his happiest and most commercial package”, and the <em>Village Voice</em>'s Richard Meltzer compared it to <em>Blood on the Tracks</em>. <em>Rolling Stone</em> was cooler.",
 "<strong>Now:</strong> it is seen as a relaxed, warm and well-played pop record, with “Crackerbox Palace” and “Beautiful Girl” as highlights."],
 beatles:[],
